.\" slosh(5) -- the file formats: configuration and layouts. Hand-written
.\" man(7), pure ASCII, like slosh.1 beside it. The settings here are held
.\" to config.c's KNOWN_TOP list by tests/test_docs.py, the same way that
.\" list is itself held to the loader -- so a knob cannot be added without
.\" this page hearing about it.
.TH SLOSH 5 "August 2026" "slosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
slosh \- configuration and layout files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $SLOSH_CONFIG
.br
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/slosh/config.kdl
.br
.I ~/.config/slosh/config.kdl
.br
.IR *.layout " files, given to " "slosh \-\-layout" " or opened as workspaces"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR slosh (1)
reads two kinds of document, sharing one syntax: a
.I configuration
(settings, colours, keys, shaders) and a
.I layout
(tabs, splits, commands \(em a session as a file).
Which schema a document is held to is decided by what is in it, not by
which flag read it: a file with
.B layout
or
.B tab
at the top is a layout, a file of settings is a configuration, and
either handed to the wrong flag is named for what it is.
.PP
No configuration file is required; every setting has a default.
.B slosh \-\-dump\-config
prints them all, as a file you could have written, and
.B slosh \-\-check
reads yours back and reports every line it could not honour.
A running session re-reads the configuration when it changes on disk: a
file that no longer parses is refused whole (the running configuration
stays), while anything smaller \(em an unknown setting, a binding that
does not parse \(em skips that line, applies the rest, and says so.
Three settings are read at pane creation rather than reload, because of
when they are needed:
.BR shell ,
.B scrollback
and
.B scrollback_bytes
apply to the next pane you open.
.SH SYNTAX
A subset of KDL (kdl.dev): one node per line, a name followed by
arguments and
.IB key = value
properties, with children in braces.
.PP
.EX
gap 1
min_pane cols=24 rows=6
keys {
    prefix "ctrl+b"           // comments run to end of line
    bind "v" "split-cols"
}
.EE
.PP
Strings are quoted with
.B \(dq
(with backslash escapes), numbers are integers, booleans are
.B true
and
.BR false ,
and
.B ~
at the start of a path is your home directory.
.SH INCLUDE
.EX
include "themes/amber.kdl"
include "keys/vim.kdl" "shaders/crt.kdl"
.EE
.PP
An included file is another configuration applied in place, so one line
swaps a theme, a key set, or a shader chain.
A relative path is relative to the file doing the including; an included
file may include others.
The includer wins wherever they disagree, and a later include wins over
an earlier one.
.B keys
blocks add to what came before;
.B shaders
and
.B states
blocks replace what they inherited.
Every included file is watched for reload.
.SH GEOMETRY
.TP
.BR gap\  \fIn\fR ,\ gap_aspect\  \fIn\fR
Space between panes, in rows, and how many columns a row is worth
(default 2, because a cell is about twice as tall as it is wide) \(em so
gaps and padding come out looking square.
.TP
.BI padding\  n\ ...
Space between a pane's frame and its contents, written the way CSS does
it: one value for every side, two for vertical/horizontal, four for
top right bottom left.
.TP
.BI rounded\  bool
Rounded frame corners.
.TP
.BI compact\  bool
Shared 1-cell borders instead of gaps: panes pack flush against divider
lines that meet in real junctions, each pane's title rides the line
above it, and dividers drag exactly as gaps did.
Interior edges give up click-to-split (the whole line is the resize
handle); floats, zoomed panes and the keyboard are unchanged.
.TP
.BR title_align\  \fIword\fR ,\ title_inset\  \fIn\fR
Where a pane's title sits on its top border
.RB ( left ,\  center ,\  right )
and how far in.
.TP
.BR min_pane\  \fRcols=\fIn\fR\ rows=\fIn\fR ,\ min_split\  \fRcols=\fIn\fR\ rows=\fIn\fR
Below
.B min_pane
a pane collapses rather than renders unusably; below
.B min_split
a split is not offered in the first place.
.SH CHROME
.TP
.BR status_bar\  \fIbool\fR ,\ status_line\  \fIbool\fR ,\ status_pad\  \fIn\fR
The tab strip along the top, the status line along the bottom, and the
line's side margin.
.TP
.BI hints\  bool
What the pointer is resting on, said in the status line.
.TP
.BI version_banner\  bool
Which build this is, in the status line when it has nothing else to say.
.TP
.BI pane_buttons\  bool
The minimise/zoom/close marks in a frame's top-right corner.
.TP
.BI bell_indicator\  bool
Whether a pane's bell shows at all (the mark, and the frame effect).
.TP
.BR zoom_mark ,\ zoom_on_mark ,\ close_mark ,\ min_mark ,\ newtab_mark ,\ bell_mark
The glyphs themselves, each one string.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.TP
.BI focus_follows_mouse\  bool
Hovering focuses \(em never at the cost of what you were doing: not
during a prefix, a drag, or a modal.
.TP
.BI multi_attach\  bool
Several terminals may attach to one session at once (the default).
Off, a new client displaces the previous one, which is the classic
\[dq]move my session here and close it there\[dq].
.TP
.BI attach_indicator\  bool
When several clients are attached, each shows a tag in its own top-right
corner: the viewer count, and on a cropped view its offset into the shared
screen
.RB ( \[dq]2\ clients\ +12+4\[dq] ),
so a view being panned by somebody else's cursor says where it is.
.TP
.BI size_follows\  active\ |\ smallest\ |\ largest
Whose size the shared screen takes when several clients are attached.
.B active
(the default) follows the newest attachment, then the last client to type or
click; smaller clients see a cursor-following crop and larger ones blank
filler.
.B smallest
is the tmux rule: nobody is cropped, the roomiest terminal pays in filler.
.B largest
gives the big display everything and the small ones a crop.
.TP
.BI ctrl_d_exits\  bool
Ctrl-D at an idle shell prompt sends
.B exit
to that shell, standing in for the EOF the tty would deliver on POSIX.
On by default on Windows, which has no VEOF and so ignores the key
entirely; off elsewhere, where the line discipline already does it.
Only with the shell itself in the foreground, on the primary screen,
and on an empty line \(em in an editor or a pager Ctrl-D is half a page
down, and a line with something on it is not an EOF.
.TP
.BI scroll_lines\  n
Rows per wheel notch.
.TP
.BR scrollback\  \fIn\fR ,\ scrollback_bytes\  \fIn\fR
Lines of history a pane keeps, and the per-pane byte ceiling that count
runs into \(em whichever bites first wins, and 0 disables that limit.
Applied to the next pane you open: shrinking history retroactively
would throw away output somebody is reading.
.TP
.BR toast_ms\  \fIn\fR ,\ splash_ms\  \fIn\fR
How long an announcement stays up, and how long the logo greets the first
attach after a session had no viewers (0 for never).
.TP
.BI hover_delay_ms\  n
How long the pointer must rest on a border before the split guide arms.
.TP
.BI select_scroll_ms\  n
A selection dragged past a pane's top or bottom edge scrolls a step
this often; each step is as many rows as the pointer is past the edge,
so the hand is the throttle and this is the metronome.
.TP
.BR double_click_ms\  \fIn\fR ,\ word_separators\  \fIstring\fR
How close two clicks must be to be a double, and what the word a
double-click selects stops at \(em listed as separators, so CJK,
accents and emoji need no table.
Whitespace always separates.
.TP
.BI anim_ms\  n
The frame clock while a shader animates.
.TP
.BR modal_scrim\  \fIn\fR ,\ dim_unfocused\  \fIn\fR ,\ float_shadow\  \fIn\fR
How far everything behind a modal is pushed back, how far the panes you
are not in are, and the shade a floating pane casts \(em each 0..255,
0 for off.
.TP
.BI keep_dead\  word
Which finished panes stay on screen offering [re-run] and [close]:
.B commands
(panes that ran a real command \(em the default),
.BR all ,
or
.B none
(collapse immediately).
.TP
.BR shell\  \fIpath\fR ,\ editor\  \fIname\fR
What a new pane runs (unset:
.BR $SHELL )
and what edits the configuration (unset:
.BR $EDITOR ,
then vi).
.TP
.BI in_band_shaders\  bool
Whether a program may set shader chains on its own pane over OSC 5577.
Off by default; clearing a chain is always allowed, because the way out
must not be the thing the setting controls.
.SH PROJECTS
.TP
.BI project_roots\  path\ ...\ \fRdepth=\fIn\fR
Where projects live, one path per argument, searched
.I depth
directories down (default 2).
Unset, the workspaces feature is dormant.
.TP
.BI project_layout\  path
A layout applied to projects that do not carry their own
.IR slosh.layout .
.SH THEME
.B theme { }
names every colour the compositor draws \(em frames, titles, tabs,
buttons, modals, toasts \(em each its own entry, 48 of them, so one can
change without dragging thirty others along.
.PP
.EX
include "~/.config/slosh/themes/phosphor.kdl"
theme { frame_focus "#00ff88" }   // ...but that one colour is mine
.EE
.PP
.B slosh \-\-dump\-config
prints the full list with the compiled-in values; ready-made themes
ship under
.IR PREFIX/share/slosh/themes/ .
.SH SHADERS
A shader is a colour pass over a pane's cells: foreground, background,
attributes \(em never the text, because rewriting text would desync
selection and copy.
A
.B shaders { }
block is a chain every pane wears, in the order written:
.PP
.EX
shaders {
    vignette amount=70
    ruler amount=60 at=80 color="#7aa2f7"
}
.EE
.PP
The built-ins:
.BR dim ,\  grayscale ,\  tint ,\  vignette ,\  gradient ,\  zebra ,\  ruler ,\  margin ,\  spotlight .
.B amount
is 0..255 (default 128); two properties work on any pass:
.B where=
.RB ( \(dqcontent\(dq
or
.BR \(dqchrome\(dq ,
the frame) and
.B channel=
.RB ( \(dqboth\(dq ,\  \(dqfg\(dq ,\  \(dqbg\(dq ).
.PP
.B amount
may be an arithmetic expression instead of a number, evaluated per
cell \(em how effects nobody built in are written without building
anything:
.PP
.EX
dim  amount="(y % 2) * 40"                        // scanlines
tint amount="255 - dist(x, y, curx, cury) * 12" color="#7aa2f7"
dim  amount="128 + sin(t / 4) / 2"                // a breathe
.EE
.PP
Variables:
.B x y cols rows
(the cell and the rect),
.B curx cury cursor focused
(the cursor and focus),
.B t since
(milliseconds, and milliseconds in the current state),
.B above below
(scrollback hidden past each edge).
Integer arithmetic and comparisons, ternaries,
.BR min ,\  max ,\  abs ,\  clamp ,\  dist ,
and
.BR sin / cos
taking degrees
.RB ( PI
is 180 \(em an angle, which is what it is here).
No loops, no recursion: a configuration cannot spin.
An expression that does not compile drops that one pass with a warning,
never the file.
.SH STATES
.B states { }
tints panes by what is
.I true
of them \(em the part you cannot discover by looking:
.PP
.EX
states {
    dead { grayscale amount=200; dim amount=90 }
    scrolled { tint amount=22 color="#7aa2f7" }
    unfocused { dim amount=60 }   // replaces dim_unfocused
}
.EE
.PP
The states, most urgent first \(em exactly one wins:
.BR dragging ,\  drop_hover ,\  drop_target ,\  dead ,\  suspended ,\  bell ,\  scrolled ,\  floating ,\  unfocused .
Naming a state replaces its default outright, including with nothing,
which is how one is turned off.
.SH KEYS
.PP
.EX
keys {
    prefix "ctrl+b"              // the leader; default ctrl+a
    bind "v" "split-cols"        // as well as the default backslash
    bind "minus" "none"          // and a default taken away
    direct {
        bind "ctrl+alt+left" "focus-left"   // no leader: fires at once
    }
}
.EE
.PP
A
.B keys
block adds to the defaults rather than replacing them.
Actions are named words \(em the same names the command palette shows,
where any of them can be run without being bound at all.
Bindings in a
.B direct
block fire without the prefix, and that chord is then gone from every
program in every pane; the cheatsheet lists them apart, so its
.I prefix then:
caption stays true.
.SH LAYOUT FILES
A session as a document \(em what
.B slosh \-\-layout
builds,
.B dump\-layout
writes, and a project checks in as
.IR slosh.layout :
.PP
.EX
layout {
    tab name="api" cwd="~/dev/api" {
        pane command="nvim"
        pane split="rows" {
            pane command="npm run dev" suspended=true
            pane
        }
    }
}
.EE
.TP
.B tab
One per tab, in strip order:
.BR name= ,
.BR cwd= ,
.B purpose=
(a label scripts and project layouts address),
.B active=true
for the tab you land in.
.TP
.B pane
A leaf, or a split when it has
.B pane
children.
.B split=
is
.B \(dqcols\(dq
or
.BR \(dqrows\(dq ;
.B weight=
is its share of the parent (left out means even).
.TP
.B command=
What the pane runs; without one, your shell.
.B cwd=
is inherited from the tab when the pane does not say; a relative path
in a layout
.I file
resolves against the file's own directory, so a checked-in layout means
the same thing from any working directory.
.TP
.B suspended=true
Laid out but running nothing until touched, showing what it would run
\(em so twelve checked-out projects are not twelve running dev servers.
.TP
.BR focus=true ,\ floating=true
The pane you start in, within its tab; a pane that starts floating,
with
.B x= y= w= h=
as its wanted rect in cells.
.PP
.B slosh \-\-check
.I file.layout
checks a layout as a layout, with the same one-problem-per-line report
the configuration gets.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/slosh/config.kdl
The configuration (see
.B SYNOPSIS
for the full search order).
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/slosh/
The annotated default configuration and an example layout, plus the
shipped themes, chrome presets and shader presets \(em each a file to
copy or
.BR include .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR slosh (1)
.PP
The prose documentation under
.IR PREFIX/share/doc/slosh/ :
the configuration, shaders, chrome, layouts and workspaces chapters are
the long form of this page.
